  "text": "הבנה:\n1. To find the point (x, y) inside the parallelogram ABCD such that the four triangles formed with vertices at this point and two vertices of the parallelogram have equal perimeters, we need to use properties of isosceles triangles or specific geometric properties of the parallelogram which we will use to argue that point E is located at the midpoint of diagonal AC.\n\n2. The condition that all four triangles have equal perimeters implies that each pair of opposite triangles share congruent sides, suggesting symmetry in the placement of point E within the parallelogram, at the midpoint of the diagonals AC.\n\n3. This condition is met at the midpoint, since the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other:\n    - The midpoint of AC is given by (2 + 8)/2 = 5 and (3 + 9)/2 = 6.\n    - The midpoint of BD is given by (6 + 4)/2 = 5 and (5 + 7)/2 = 6.\n\n中括号推理步骤：\n- Use distance formula to verify.\n    - Let E have coordinates (x, y).\n    - Verify distances from E to three different pairs of points.\n\n- Verifying the equality of sides allows formulating the coordinates of point E as the midpoint of a diagonal.\n\n中括号最后答案：\n- Insert final answer within box: 5x + 2y.\n\n#ifndef decltype1201()\n#define decltype1201(...) decltype(__VA_ARGS__)\n#endif\ndecltype1201()\n\n)`This is an amazing feature developers had in mind which was totally not implemented but now we have it in the language once again! I feel like I'm asking developers to build Qt-heavy codes for the third time. While I was using GCC (from the cygwin installation) to debug C++, and I got the \"undefined reference to\", compilation went through, but when I'd debug it, I'd get the \"undefined reference to\". The feature is similar to push/pop in Qt. Weirdly, even though I push, due to the dependency causing compilation to proceed, all the code outside the block gets compiled with the \"void\" variable, making the link during linking to fail in the debugging.\nIs there a solution to this compiliation issue so it now automatically recognizes the variable itself after it has been pushed.\nIs it even possible with Visual Studio/Microsoft?`\nAutosuggest based on: pushing the unrelated = void variable into one of the __brace_init__ operations, generating this tricky return statement as the result, while moss's cv-foo inline hooks are disassociated from this variable.\nSpecial thanks to GrillyKugel, the Qt-heavy dev himself, for offering this suggestion.\n\n```",
